{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794534749778,"sessionID":"ses_e2d67e4d1ffe4lBa28HlX5551k","part":{"partID":"prt_1d29b30e7001snyz1u4No3wdOr","sessionID":"ses_e2d67e4d1ffe4lBa28HlX5551k","messageID":"msg_1d29b1cc70016XzcGE53AQt7UC","type":"tool","id":"call_c241b1c108ee465eb2fcd414","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md","offset":1,"limit":120},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md, lines 1-120\n1: ---\n2: title: Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n3: description: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code including naming principles, commenting philosophy, and reference comment patterns.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode universal patterns\"\n6:   - \"cross language naming principles\"\n7:   - \"commenting philosophy why\"\n8:   - \"reference comment patterns\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.1.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n15: \n16: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: This reference defines patterns that apply universally across JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and JSON/JSONC in the OpenCode codebase. These principles ensure consistency regardless of language choice.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Before writing any new OpenCode system code\n29: - When reviewing code for consistency\n30: - When onboarding to OpenCode development patterns\n31: - As the foundation before applying language-specific rules\n32: \n33: ### Core Principle\n34: \n35: > **Clarity over cleverness.** Code is read far more often than written. Optimize for the reader, not the writer.\n36: \n37: ### Key Sources (Evidence)\n38: \n39: | Pattern | Source File | Line Reference |\n40: |---------|-------------|----------------|\n41: | Reference comments | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts` | Near file top and import blocks |\n42: | Section organization | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts` | Numbered divider blocks |\n43: | Naming conventions | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts` | Throughout |\n44: | Python docstrings | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Module and function docstrings |\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. NAMING PRINCIPLES\n49: \n50: ### Descriptive Names\n51: \n52: Names should describe what something IS or DOES without requiring context:\n53: \n54: **Good** (JS/TS use camelCase, Python/Shell use snake_case):\n55: ```javascript\n56: // JavaScript\n57: calculateMemoryScore()\n58: validateInputLengths()\n59: handleMemorySearch()\n60: ```\n61: ```typescript\n62: // TypeScript (same as JS for functions; PascalCase for types)\n63: calculateMemoryScore()\n64: validateInputLengths()\n65: interface SearchResult { }\n66: type MemoryState = 'active' | 'archived';\n67: ```\n68: ```python\n69: # Python\n70: calculate_memory_score()\n71: validate_input_lengths()\n72: handle_trigger_lookup()\n73: ```\n74: \n75: **Bad**:\n76: ```\n77: calcScore()\n78: validateLengths()\n79: handle()\n80: ```\n81: \n82: ### Avoid Abbreviations\n83: \n84: Spell out words unless industry-standard:\n85: \n86: | Use | Don't Use | Exception |\n87: |-----|-----------|-----------|\n88: | `configuration` | `cfg` | - |\n89: | `database` | `db` | OK for `DB` in constants |\n90: | `message` | `msg` | - |\n91: | `request` | `req` | OK in route handlers |\n92: | `response` | `res` | OK in route handlers |\n93: | `HTTP` | `http` | OK - industry standard |\n94: | `URL` | `url` | OK - industry standard |\n95: | `ID` | `id` | OK - industry standard |\n96: \n97: ### Consistency Rule\n98: \n99: Same concept = same name across the entire codebase:\n100: \n101: ```javascript\n102: // GOOD: Consistent naming (JS/TS)\n103: memorySearch()          // in handlers\n104: handleMemorySearch()    // wrapper\n105: MemorySearchResult      // type/interface (PascalCase)\n106: \n107: // BAD: Inconsistent naming\n108: memorySearch()          // in handlers\n109: handle_search()         // wrapper - wrong convention!\n110: SearchMemoryResult      // type - different word order!\n111: ```\n112: \n113: ### Boolean Naming\n114: \n115: Booleans should read as questions:\n116: \n117: ```javascript\n118: // JavaScript / TypeScript (camelCase)\n119: isValid\n120: hasResults\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 121]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md, lines 1-120\n1: ---\n2: title: Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n3: description: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code including naming principles, commenting philosophy, and reference comment patterns.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode universal patterns\"\n6:   - \"cross language naming principles\"\n7:   - \"commenting philosophy why\"\n8:   - \"reference comment patterns\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.1.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Universal Patterns - Cross-Language Code Standards\n15: \n16: Patterns applicable to ALL languages in OpenCode system code.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: This reference defines patterns that apply universally across JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and JSON/JSONC in the OpenCode codebase. These principles ensure consistency regardless of language choice.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Before writing any new OpenCode system code\n29: - When reviewing code for consistency\n30: - When onboarding to OpenCode development patterns\n31: - As the foundation before applying language-specific rules\n32: \n33: ### Core Principle\n34: \n35: > **Clarity over cleverness.** Code is read far more often than written. Optimize for the reader, not the writer.\n36: \n37: ### Key Sources (Evidence)\n38: \n39: | Pattern | Source File | Line Reference |\n40: |---------|-------------|----------------|\n41: | Reference comments | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts` | Near file top and import blocks |\n42: | Section organization | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts` | Numbered divider blocks |\n43: | Naming conventions | `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts` | Throughout |\n44: | Python docstrings | `.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` | Module and function docstrings |\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. NAMING PRINCIPLES\n49: \n50: ### Descriptive Names\n51: \n52: Names should describe what something IS or DOES without requiring context:\n53: \n54: **Good** (JS/TS use camelCase, Python/Shell use snake_case):\n55: ```javascript\n56: // JavaScript\n57: calculateMemoryScore()\n58: validateInputLengths()\n59: handleMemorySearch()\n60: ```\n61: ```typescript\n62: // TypeScript (same as JS for functions; PascalCase for types)\n63: calculateMemoryScore()\n64: validateInputLengths()\n65: interface SearchResult { }\n66: type MemoryState = 'active' | 'archived';\n67: ```\n68: ```python\n69: # Python\n70: calculate_memory_score()\n71: validate_input_lengths()\n72: handle_trigger_lookup()\n73: ```\n74: \n75: **Bad**:\n76: ```\n77: calcScore()\n78: validateLengths()\n79: handle()\n80: ```\n81: \n82: ### Avoid Abbreviations\n83: \n84: Spell out words unless industry-standard:\n85: \n86: | Use | Don't Use | Exception |\n87: |-----|-----------|-----------|\n88: | `configuration` | `cfg` | - |\n89: | `database` | `db` | OK for `DB` in constants |\n90: | `message` | `msg` | - |\n91: | `request` | `req` | OK in route handlers |\n92: | `response` | `res` | OK in route handlers |\n93: | `HTTP` | `http` | OK - industry standard |\n94: | `URL` | `url` | OK - industry standard |\n95: | `ID` | `id` | OK - industry standard |\n96: \n97: ### Consistency Rule\n98: \n99: Same concept = same name across the entire codebase:\n100: \n101: ```javascript\n102: // GOOD: Consistent naming (JS/TS)\n103: memorySearch()          // in handlers\n104: handleMemorySearch()    // wrapper\n105: MemorySearchResult      // type/interface (PascalCase)\n106: \n107: // BAD: Inconsistent naming\n108: memorySearch()          // in handlers\n109: handle_search()         // wrapper - wrong convention!\n110: SearchMemoryResult      // type - different word order!\n111: ```\n112: \n113: ### Boolean Naming\n114: \n115: Booleans should read as questions:\n116: \n117: ```javascript\n118: // JavaScript / TypeScript (camelCase)\n119: isValid\n120: hasResults\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 121]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":120}"},"time":{"start":1794534748391,"end":1794534749778}}}}
