{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794534767513,"sessionID":"ses_e2d67e4d1ffe4lBa28HlX5551k","part":{"partID":"prt_1d29b78d0001WBzJyqM3F8d02X","sessionID":"ses_e2d67e4d1ffe4lBa28HlX5551k","messageID":"msg_1d29b6969001si7gx0KK8lYpkq","type":"tool","id":"call_5166ffd77bf7403ab5ca1560","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":120,"offset":150,"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md, lines 150-269\n150: The universal style guide asks each surface to state its identifier scheme. This is the SKILLED one:\n151: \n152: | Language | Identifier convention |\n153: | --- | --- |\n154: | JavaScript and TypeScript | `camelCase` for variables and functions, `PascalCase` for classes and types, `UPPER_SNAKE` for constants |\n155: | Python and Shell | `snake_case` for variables and functions, `PascalCase` for classes, `UPPER_SNAKE` for constants |\n156: \n157: The language checklists under `../../../assets/checklists/` enforce it.\n158: \n159: ### Plural Naming for Collections\n160: \n161: Arrays and collections use plural names:\n162: \n163: ```\n164: // GOOD\n165: const memories = [];\n166: const results = searchResults.items;\n167: for (const entry of entries) { ... }\n168: \n169: // BAD\n170: const memory = [];        // misleading - it's an array\n171: const result = results.items;  // confusing\n172: for (const entry of entry) { ... }  // very confusing\n173: ```\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 3. COMMENTING PHILOSOPHY\n178: \n179: ### Core Principles\n180: \n181: 1. **Quantity limit (Skilled surface setting):** Maximum 3 comments per 10 lines of code. This number is the Skilled surface's budget. The comment-density rule it applies, and the rule that each surface sets its own budget, live in the shared [code style guide](../../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md).\n182: 2. **Focus on purposeful semantics:** Explain WHY something is done, not WHAT it does\n183: 3. **No commented-out code:** Delete unused code (git preserves history)\n184: 4. **Function purpose comments:** Single line above function describing intent\n185: 5. **Capitalization:** Every inline comment sentence MUST start with a capital letter\n186:    - `// Process items in reverse order` (correct)\n187:    - `// process items in reverse order` (wrong)\n188:    - Exception: `eslint-disable`, `@ts-` directives, and technical identifiers (e.g., `// e.g. ...`)\n189: \n190: ### WHY Not WHAT\n191: \n192: Comments explain **reasoning**, not **mechanics**:\n193: \n194: **Bad - describes WHAT**:\n195: ```javascript\n196: // Loop through items\n197: for (const item of items) { ... }\n198: \n199: // Increment counter\n200: counter++;\n201: \n202: // Check if valid\n203: if (isValid) { ... }\n204: ```\n205: \n206: **Good - explains WHY**:\n207: ```javascript\n208: // Process in reverse order for dependency resolution\n209: for (const item of items.reverse()) { ... }\n210: \n211: // Track retry count for exponential backoff control\n212: retryCount++;\n213: \n214: // Skip invalid entries to avoid downstream embedding errors\n215: if (!isValid) return null;\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### When to Comment\n219: \n220: | Situation | Comment? | Example |\n221: |-----------|----------|---------|\n222: | Non-obvious logic | YES | `// Sort timestamp DESC so newest appears first` |\n223: | Business rule | YES | `// Constitutional tier must always surface regardless of score` |\n224: | Workaround | YES | `// Works around an SDK hang on empty payloads` |\n225: | Security concern | YES | `// SEC: sanitize input to prevent stored XSS (CWE-79)` |\n226: | Self-explanatory code | NO | `const sum = a + b;` needs no comment |\n227: | Obvious loops | NO | `for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++)` needs no comment |\n228: \n229: ### Header Markers\n230: \n231: SKILLED TypeScript files carry a `MODULE:` header where the TypeScript checklist requires one, and the alignment verifier checks that marker. Header forms for the other languages are in each language's style guide.\n232: \n233: ### Intentional-Simplification Marker (`ceiling:`)\n234: \n235: The universal style guide lets a surface define a marker for a deliberate shortcut. SKILLED defines `ceiling:`. Use the neutral `ceiling:` prefix and name the shortcut, its known ceiling and the upgrade path or trigger:\n236: \n237: ```python\n238: # ceiling: global lock; switch to per-account locks if throughput matters\n239: ```\n240: \n241: This is durable WHY, the constraint plus the decision, so the ephemeral-pointer rule allows it: it names no tracking artifact. Two constraints:\n242: \n243: - Use a neutral prefix (`ceiling:` or `intentional-limit:`), never a tool or brand name. A brand prefix reads as a perishable, cargo-cult label.\n244: - Do NOT add `ceiling:` to the comment-hygiene checker's allowed-pattern list. It already passes, because it contains no forbidden id, and allow-listing it would let a forbidden id on the same line slip through, because allowed patterns short-circuit violation detection.\n245: \n246: To list every marker in the tracked code and flag the ones whose trigger can never fire, run `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/scripts/ceiling-report.sh`.\n247: \n248: ### Comment Placement\n249: \n250: Place comments on the line BEFORE the code they describe:\n251: \n252: ```javascript\n253: // GOOD: Comment before\n254: // Calculate weighted score using decay function\n255: const score = calculateDecay(baseScore, age);\n256: \n257: // BAD: Comment after\n258: const score = calculateDecay(baseScore, age); // weighted decay\n259: ```\n260: \n261: ---\n262: \n263: ## 4. REFERENCE COMMENT PATTERNS\n264: \n265: > **Governed by the canonical rule.** Comments must never embed an ephemeral-artifact pointer: a spec folder/number, a packet/phase/task/checklist/requirement id, a feature-catalog entry, an ADR id, or a ticket id. See [`../../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) §4 \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\" for the allowed-vs-forbidden contract. Only a **durable** external standard may be cited in a comment.\n266: \n267: ### Hard-Block Comment-Hygiene Gate\n268: \n269: Comment hygiene is not a soft style preference in OpenCode system code. Ephemeral-artifact pointers are a HARD-BLOCK gate for code comments: do not write spec-folder paths, ADR ids, requirement ids, checklist ids, task ids, packet/phase ids, or ticket-local references into comments. Replace them with the durable WHY that will still be true after a packet is renamed, archived, or deleted.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 270]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md, lines 150-269\n150: The universal style guide asks each surface to state its identifier scheme. This is the SKILLED one:\n151: \n152: | Language | Identifier convention |\n153: | --- | --- |\n154: | JavaScript and TypeScript | `camelCase` for variables and functions, `PascalCase` for classes and types, `UPPER_SNAKE` for constants |\n155: | Python and Shell | `snake_case` for variables and functions, `PascalCase` for classes, `UPPER_SNAKE` for constants |\n156: \n157: The language checklists under `../../../assets/checklists/` enforce it.\n158: \n159: ### Plural Naming for Collections\n160: \n161: Arrays and collections use plural names:\n162: \n163: ```\n164: // GOOD\n165: const memories = [];\n166: const results = searchResults.items;\n167: for (const entry of entries) { ... }\n168: \n169: // BAD\n170: const memory = [];        // misleading - it's an array\n171: const result = results.items;  // confusing\n172: for (const entry of entry) { ... }  // very confusing\n173: ```\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 3. COMMENTING PHILOSOPHY\n178: \n179: ### Core Principles\n180: \n181: 1. **Quantity limit (Skilled surface setting):** Maximum 3 comments per 10 lines of code. This number is the Skilled surface's budget. The comment-density rule it applies, and the rule that each surface sets its own budget, live in the shared [code style guide](../../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md).\n182: 2. **Focus on purposeful semantics:** Explain WHY something is done, not WHAT it does\n183: 3. **No commented-out code:** Delete unused code (git preserves history)\n184: 4. **Function purpose comments:** Single line above function describing intent\n185: 5. **Capitalization:** Every inline comment sentence MUST start with a capital letter\n186:    - `// Process items in reverse order` (correct)\n187:    - `// process items in reverse order` (wrong)\n188:    - Exception: `eslint-disable`, `@ts-` directives, and technical identifiers (e.g., `// e.g. ...`)\n189: \n190: ### WHY Not WHAT\n191: \n192: Comments explain **reasoning**, not **mechanics**:\n193: \n194: **Bad - describes WHAT**:\n195: ```javascript\n196: // Loop through items\n197: for (const item of items) { ... }\n198: \n199: // Increment counter\n200: counter++;\n201: \n202: // Check if valid\n203: if (isValid) { ... }\n204: ```\n205: \n206: **Good - explains WHY**:\n207: ```javascript\n208: // Process in reverse order for dependency resolution\n209: for (const item of items.reverse()) { ... }\n210: \n211: // Track retry count for exponential backoff control\n212: retryCount++;\n213: \n214: // Skip invalid entries to avoid downstream embedding errors\n215: if (!isValid) return null;\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### When to Comment\n219: \n220: | Situation | Comment? | Example |\n221: |-----------|----------|---------|\n222: | Non-obvious logic | YES | `// Sort timestamp DESC so newest appears first` |\n223: | Business rule | YES | `// Constitutional tier must always surface regardless of score` |\n224: | Workaround | YES | `// Works around an SDK hang on empty payloads` |\n225: | Security concern | YES | `// SEC: sanitize input to prevent stored XSS (CWE-79)` |\n226: | Self-explanatory code | NO | `const sum = a + b;` needs no comment |\n227: | Obvious loops | NO | `for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++)` needs no comment |\n228: \n229: ### Header Markers\n230: \n231: SKILLED TypeScript files carry a `MODULE:` header where the TypeScript checklist requires one, and the alignment verifier checks that marker. Header forms for the other languages are in each language's style guide.\n232: \n233: ### Intentional-Simplification Marker (`ceiling:`)\n234: \n235: The universal style guide lets a surface define a marker for a deliberate shortcut. SKILLED defines `ceiling:`. Use the neutral `ceiling:` prefix and name the shortcut, its known ceiling and the upgrade path or trigger:\n236: \n237: ```python\n238: # ceiling: global lock; switch to per-account locks if throughput matters\n239: ```\n240: \n241: This is durable WHY, the constraint plus the decision, so the ephemeral-pointer rule allows it: it names no tracking artifact. Two constraints:\n242: \n243: - Use a neutral prefix (`ceiling:` or `intentional-limit:`), never a tool or brand name. A brand prefix reads as a perishable, cargo-cult label.\n244: - Do NOT add `ceiling:` to the comment-hygiene checker's allowed-pattern list. It already passes, because it contains no forbidden id, and allow-listing it would let a forbidden id on the same line slip through, because allowed patterns short-circuit violation detection.\n245: \n246: To list every marker in the tracked code and flag the ones whose trigger can never fire, run `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/scripts/ceiling-report.sh`.\n247: \n248: ### Comment Placement\n249: \n250: Place comments on the line BEFORE the code they describe:\n251: \n252: ```javascript\n253: // GOOD: Comment before\n254: // Calculate weighted score using decay function\n255: const score = calculateDecay(baseScore, age);\n256: \n257: // BAD: Comment after\n258: const score = calculateDecay(baseScore, age); // weighted decay\n259: ```\n260: \n261: ---\n262: \n263: ## 4. REFERENCE COMMENT PATTERNS\n264: \n265: > **Governed by the canonical rule.** Comments must never embed an ephemeral-artifact pointer: a spec folder/number, a packet/phase/task/checklist/requirement id, a feature-catalog entry, an ADR id, or a ticket id. See [`../../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) §4 \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\" for the allowed-vs-forbidden contract. Only a **durable** external standard may be cited in a comment.\n266: \n267: ### Hard-Block Comment-Hygiene Gate\n268: \n269: Comment hygiene is not a soft style preference in OpenCode system code. Ephemeral-artifact pointers are a HARD-BLOCK gate for code comments: do not write spec-folder paths, ADR ids, requirement ids, checklist ids, task ids, packet/phase ids, or ticket-local references into comments. Replace them with the durable WHY that will still be true after a packet is renamed, archived, or deleted.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 270]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\":120,\"offset\":150,\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794534766800,"end":1794534767513}}}}
